<ClinicNote slug="zone-2-training">
  <p>Exercise intensity is often split into five zones, from very easy (zone 1) to all-out (zone 5). Zone 2 is the steady, comfortable effort you could hold for a long time: a brisk walk, an easy jog, a relaxed ride.</p>
  <p>In 2025, a panel of endurance-science experts (including Stephen Seiler, one of the best-known researchers on endurance training intensity) agreed on a definition:</p>
  <blockquote><p>Zone 2 sits at "intensities located immediately below the first lactate or ventilatory threshold."</p><cite>Sitko et al., Int J Sports Physiol Perform, 2025 [1]</cite></blockquote>
  <h2>How to find it without a lab</h2>
  <p>Use the talk test. You can speak in full sentences, but you'd rather not sing. Breathing is deeper than at rest, but steady. If you're gasping between words, ease off.</p>
  <h2>Is it magic? No.</h2>
  <p>Zone 2 builds an aerobic base you can repeat day after day without wrecking your body, which is why we love it. But a 2025 review found it isn't the single best intensity for fitness:</p>
  <blockquote><p>"Evidence suggests prioritizing higher exercise intensities (&gt; Zone 2) is critical to maximize cardiometabolic health."</p><cite>Storoschuk et al., Sports Medicine, 2025 [2]</cite></blockquote>
  <p>The practical answer is both: plenty of easy, steady work, plus some harder efforts each week. Australia's guidelines recommend 2.5 to 5 hours of moderate activity a week, plus muscle strengthening on at least two days [3].</p>
  <h2>Why we named the clinic after it</h2>
  <p>Zone 2 is foundational, sustainable effort, done consistently. That's how we think about recovery too. Slow is fast.</p>

